Oh, well, that's nice.

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:51.060
I didn't ask for your hand. Right one or left one.

00:16:51.430 --> 00:16:53.710
Now, let's just get this straight.

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:57.000
You've got to wind up with Priscilla in the end.

00:16:57.690 --> 00:16:59.130
History demands it.

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:11.400
to wind up that way, I'm winding up with

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:13.500
Tide for Procter and Gamble, demand

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:27.500
good. Elton's Film Scrapbook

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:28.760
presents

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:31.990
Weepi Talks Turkey.

00:17:34.550 --> 00:17:35.590
I don't know.

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:38.520
Tell you what I've got you in here for.

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:42.280
Now, before we go knock off, kick off Aunt Lucy's,

00:17:44.810 --> 00:17:46.610
we're going to talk turkey here.

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:50.520
Now, last year, they were over here.

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:51.820
And what happened?

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:54.930
They marched right down our table for a touchdown

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:56.490
in the first minute of play.

00:17:57.680 --> 00:17:59.850
I didn't need you to sniff a bowl.

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:04.820
How did they do it? With a deception play.

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:07.800
Right in the middle of the prayer.

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:11.440
The referee started to...

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:15.450
They didn't like it anyhow.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:22.890
Anyhow, they got all of the white meat, all the dark meat.

00:18:23.830 --> 00:18:24.810
And what did we get?

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.440
You bet we did.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.060
I'll tell you what I want you to do.

00:18:31.180 --> 00:18:33.100
I want you to get in and block those potatoes.

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:35.890
They'll be lumpy anyhow, but block them.

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:40.920
Another thing I want you to keep in mind is really eat,

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:43.460
because all it counts is who's got the most bones

00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:44.840
after it's over, you see?

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:47.780
Now we're going in and we're going to eat,

00:18:49.780 --> 00:18:51.120
and we're going to eat hard.

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:55.200
Try passing the butter, son.

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:56.580
See how good you are.

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:21.900
It's all right son. I cut my finger. That's a good boy. I knew you'd get it out pretty soon

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:28.200
Now when we get over there the first thing I want you to do this is later

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:32.960
I want you to spill coffee all over the table, Claude.

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:35.890
I understand they're no good on a wet field.

00:19:36.850 --> 00:19:39.640
All right now, Tom, let's hope it goes.

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:41.800
Wait just a moment, Tom.

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:47.120
You know better than to hit me like that.

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:50.620
Gee, that feels good.

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:59.590
Oh, the poor hen that laid that thing.

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:11.070
I want to ask you, what are you going to do if Uncle Wilbur, Wilbur,

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:15.250
Uncle Wilbur, he's the one with the wobble.

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:18.310
Oh, my.

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:20.950
What do you do when he reaches for a drumstick?

00:20:21.380 --> 00:20:24.710
Well, Coach, I think I reach for the succotash

00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:26.270
and block him out with an elbow like that.

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:29.970
I think this is too...

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:30.760
physical to you?

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:34.530
Or I say, maybe it's like that.

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:39.890
Well, that's good.

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:41.070
Bite clean, boy.

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:41.790
Bite clean.

00:20:43.060 --> 00:20:45.440
Now I want to tell you what I want you to do.

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:49.980
When you go down for those cranberry sauce,

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:52.080
make it a clean play.

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:54.100
Don't come out looking like the Crimson Tide.

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:56.160
Oh, Tide.

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:06.810
Please, I've got a headache.

00:21:08.810 --> 00:21:11.050
Hey, Pop, what about me? What do I do?

00:21:11.250 --> 00:21:13.290
Yeah, what about you? What do you do?

00:21:13.950 --> 00:21:17.070
Well, you're the shortest, so you can run the interference force.

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:19.890
All right, now, supposing I've just got a full plate.

00:21:20.140 --> 00:21:22.840
What do you do? Hurry, boy, faster. They're moving in on you.

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:24.100
There you are. That's the point.

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:27.060
Hey, don't knock it out of them too soon.

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:29.960
Okay, you can go.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:34.880
back the hard way. Now what do we do if they reach for a wing?

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:36.320
Try the wing back play.

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:38.300
Yeah, let's try that. That makes them move older.

00:21:40.330 --> 00:21:43.200
Now, to make more room, we can get more food.

00:21:43.660 --> 00:21:45.600
Now we're gonna try the old 42 play.

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:48.830
What do you think, Pop? You think they're in condition?

00:21:48.890 --> 00:21:52.490
They're in condition, Pop. I've had them on a nine-day diet for nine weeks.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.000
You don't have a Clark Gable calendar, do you?

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:05.200
I have a Marilyn Monroe calendar.

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:08.600
And which one of you came in and drew a mustache on her head?

00:22:08.960 --> 00:22:13.310
All right, you ready?

00:22:13.590 --> 00:22:15.610
Now we're gonna try the old 42 play.

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:16.750
All right?

00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:20.930
You're outside.

00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:24.690
You're outside.

00:22:25.670 --> 00:22:27.830
I don't think you know much about this game, do you?

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:29.980
You haven't even-

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:34.700
He hasn't eaten in weeks. I've got him trained, Pa.

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:38.520
Yeah? Sort of a one-man's famine, huh?

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:43.420
So thin that he could slice a turkey with his shoulder blade.

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:46.940
Good. We'll let him crawl in and work from the inside.

00:22:47.780 --> 00:22:52.480
What do they do when they get in the huddle and they call out all those numbers?

00:22:52.660 --> 00:22:54.480
Wait, what do you know if they're game or not?

00:22:55.770 --> 00:22:59.020
Well, they call out those numbers to fool the other team.

00:22:59.160 --> 00:22:59.960
See, they're gonna call out four...

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.980
Fourteen, sixteen, twenty-two. One time the egg on sixteen, the next time on twenty-two.

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:05.940
Oh, you blabber mouth!

00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:10.840
All right, now, we're gonna get ready to go over there.

00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:16.000
I want you to get in there and eat. Cut, slice, but don't chew, just swallow.

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:18.940
Okay, Pop, we'll be all a-biting.

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:26.470
Too soon.

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:29.800
All right, off to the showers.

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:33.820
Now, when that whistle blows, I want you to come out bitin'.

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:42.780
Come in here. I'm ashamed of you.

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:45.480
I'm glad I wasn't the first one out.

00:23:45.950 --> 00:23:47.310
Now, go pick up your mother.

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:59.600
You think we got a chance winding up in the gravy bowl this year?

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:02.570
It's in the bag.

00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:04.950
Good. You get over there.

00:24:06.620 --> 00:24:08.040
Well, there's one they couldn't cook for.

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:12.310
Boys, we know who's in back of us.

00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:14.500
We know who our inspiration was.

00:24:15.710 --> 00:24:17.770
What do you say we hoister on our shoulders, huh?

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:19.010
What do you say, boy?

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:20.550
Okay, Tim. Let's go.

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:27.770
Thank you very much.

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:29.880
Now I have a little something.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.090
to say in behalf of our American home.

00:24:34.020 --> 00:24:37.980
In the year of 1620, a group of hearty, determined people

00:24:37.980 --> 00:24:40.720
crossed the Atlantic in quest of the land

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:44.330
where they might live freely and worship above a whisper.

00:24:45.080 --> 00:24:47.500
These pilgrims found such a home known

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:49.600
as the United States of America.

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:52.260
And the history tells us that our forefathers

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:55.140
gave humble thanks to God on the last Thursday of November

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:57.420
of that year for their deliverance.

00:24:58.220 --> 00:24:59.980
To me, this Thanksgiving...

00:25:01.640 --> 00:25:08.410
332 years later is one of the most important thanksgiving we'll ever celebrate and when we

00:25:08.410 --> 00:25:16.530
offer thanks this time it's also ask God to give us the strength to keep a firm hold on the freedom

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:23.510
which we are now being threatened if this country is conquered by aggressors there can be no other

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:29.850
pilgrimage where there will be no more free lands they may go to now on behalf

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:36.560
of our sponsors Tide, the advertising agencies and the entire cast, may we wish

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:40.650
you a very pleasant Thanksgiving. Good night.

